\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The center of the lifted group}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In even dimension the center of the
\vocabk{lift of the signed coordinate group}{fdip-016P} is its
two-element kernel. In odd dimension the center also contains
the two signed products #{\pm f_1\cdots f_n} and no other
elements. These two project to total inversion and satisfy
##{(\pm f_1\cdots f_n)^2
   =(-1)^{n(n+1)/2}\bbI_S.}}

\proof{
\p{Use the faithful word group of \ref{fdip-016U}. A central
word commutes in particular with every #{d_j}. For an even
monomial only the empty subset survives this condition, as in
\ref{fdip-016U}. For an odd monomial any absent #{j} gives
conjugation sign minus one. The only possible odd monomial
therefore uses every generator, which requires #{n} odd.
The monomial basis and the definite parity of the word rule out
a mixture of the two possibilities. The full product in odd
dimension commutes with each generator, hence with the entire
algebra. It belongs to the word group and projects to the
product of the single axis flips. Dividing any odd central word
by it leaves a scalar word, hence a sign by \ref{fdip-016U}.
Squaring the product uses #{n(n-1)/2} interchanges and #{n}
negative squares. The faithful representation carries this value
to the displayed scalar. For #{n=1} this gives the cyclic group
of order #{4} in \ref{fdip-016Q}.}
}
